  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Build 746\n\nThe recent updates that removed the ability to broadcast triggers from objects that are enabled locally but disabled remotely:  this is a HUGE issue that is going to be a major problem for non-advanced users. \n\n It is often the only way to properly sync triggers without using an intermediate Custom trigger on an empty gameobject, which is not something that most new worldbuilders know how to do.\n\nExample: a game of whack-a-mole.  Each player picks up their mallet and locally enables their mole collider.  As they whack moles, they add one to their own score and then broadcast that score.  With the current build, no one else will receive the score broadcast.\n\nSecond example:  a station such as a DJ booth has the colliders on the buttons enabled locally with a single button, so that only one person at a time can use the booth.  All the button results are now not broadcast.\n\nThese can be worked around with Custom triggers, but laboriously and non-intuitively.\nSync is already very difficult.  This update made it doubly so.\n\nAnother thing that happens is that people use \"trigger occlusions\" for better or for worse.  So if you are not in a part of the map, triggers locally enable and disable gameobjects for performance reasons.  Now people in different parts of the map will do actions they think are synced but are not, depending on where other people are, not even just late joiners!\n\nIn fact...how does this work for late joiners?  If a trigger is broadcast ABO, does it fire or not for a late joiner??  Only if the gameobject is active for them on load?\nIf the buffered triggers are sequential, this starts to matter !! Let's say you have an SetGameObjectActive on AlwaysBufferOne, that is enabled and disabled a number of times.  It has an OnEnable trigger.  When the late joiner loads, do they get the trigger?  It WILL DEPEND on the state of the object when they load?  This makes no sense.\n\nThis means you should NEVER have a trigger on an object that gets disabled.  Since objects get enabled/disabled a lot for various visual and logic reasons, this effectively means that EVERY SINGLE TRIGGER will need to call to an empty with Customs on it.  This is not the desired workflow for anyone, let alone new worldbuilders.",
